{# AN ADD OR EDIT SURFACE, AND IT OWNS ITS FIELDS. Four kinds of node used to be
   assembled by hand in the scene files — a separated lead row, two borderless
   lines, a wrapping row of peer pills, an action row — and the surface is the
   thing that knows how they go together, so it says it once. That is the same
   move `table` makes for its rows, applied to input rather than output.

   THE CONTRACT IS NOT A LIST OF FIELDS, DELIBERATELY. The widget grammar in
   `designbook/design-system/action-pattern.md` is a RULE — "every add, edit and
   organisation surface uses it without variation" — and its first line rejects
   the shape a field list would produce: "The dialog is not a form of labelled
   rows." A flat `fields[]` array would let a caller build three text lines, or
   two prominent properties, or a column of labelled rows. `lead` + `lines` +
   `pills` + `actions` cannot express any of them.

   THE FOUR REGIONS ARE SLOTS, AND SINCE BB-420 TASK 20 THEY ARE SLOTS RATHER
   THAN PROPS. The reason is the create-ticket dialog, whose fields are real
   Drupal field widgets: a widget RENDERS itself, and rendered content cannot go
   into a prop. So the caller renders each field — a real form element
   (`form_input` / `form_textarea`, since BB-420 Task 31 retired the bespoke
   `text_line`) for a line, `property_pill` for a peer or a lead — and this
   component places what it is handed.

   WHAT THAT COST, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. `lead` was an OBJECT before, and
   its singularity was carried by the type: grammar 3a allows at most one
   emphasised property per surface, because "a surface where everything is
   emphasised emphasises nothing". A slot cannot carry a cardinality — core's own
   slot definition admits only `title`, `description` and `examples`, and
   `ComponentValidator` does not validate slot content at all. The rule did not
   become a comment: it moved to the one place that can still check it, the FORM
   DISPLAY, where `BbTicketCreateForm` refuses to build a surface whose display
   configures two fields with `surface_region: lead`, and a kernel test pins that
   refusal. `lead_label` is a prop for the same reason — the row names itself
   from the property it holds, and with `lead` rendered by the caller the label
   is no longer readable from it.

   "NO FLAT FIELD LIST" SURVIVES THE CHANGE UNTOUCHED: four NAMED slots keep it
   exactly as four named props did. A caller still cannot put a second pill in
   the lead row, stack the pills into a column, or turn a line into a labelled
   row — there is no slot that would take it.

   THE TWO RULES THAT MOVED OUT WITH THEIR MARKUP, restated because this is where
   they are enforced:

     "a large title line and a smaller description line beneath it. The line
      carries its identity in its placeholder."   → `form_input` / `form_textarea`
      (BB-420 Task 31 retired `text_line`; the "no box, no border" half of
      this rule did not survive the retirement — every editable field is now a
      real form element, which carries its own visible border)

     "the row is a rule: every peer property is a pill in ONE row, it wraps
      rather than stacks, and beyond roughly six pills the low-frequency ones
      collapse behind an overflow control that will belong here."  → the pill row
                                                                     below

   No overflow control is rendered today, and that is the second rule's own
   stance rather than an omission: the create surface carries three peer pills
   plus the lead, which fit in one row, and a `…` that opens nothing is a worse
   lie than an absent affordance.

   WHERE THE ACTION ROW IS. `dialog` owns the hull — a title row, then `message`,
   `body` and `actions` in a bordered footer — so a form that is a dialog's body
   leaves `actions` empty and the hull renders them. `actions` here is for a form
   that is its own surface, and it is what keeps "exactly one submit plus cancel"
   a property of the form rather than of whatever happens to contain it.

   A SURFACE OF ONE LINE HAS NO WRAPPER — and that is the line's own job now, not
   a branch of this file. The one caller that renders a single line is
   `page_header`'s editable heading, which keeps the field INSIDE the `<h1>`
   because an editable heading is still the screen's heading, and an `<h1>` takes
   phrasing content, so an `<input>` is legal there and a `<div>` is not. That
   caller renders `form_input` directly (BB-420 Task 31; formerly
   `text_line`); a surface is what you reach for when there is more than one
   thing to place. #}
<div{{ (attributes ?: create_attribute()).addClass(['form', 'flex', 'flex-col', 'gap-3']) }}>
  {#- THE LEAD ROW, grammar 3a. It is above the lines and separated from them by a
      rule, because the property that decides what the rest of the surface even
      means is not one of the surface's peers. Making it merely the first pill was
      tried and was not enough — first in a row still reads as "the first of
      these", and it is not one of these. The row names itself from the property
      it holds, which is what `lead_label` is: one name for a row of one
      property. #}
  {% set lead_content %}{% block lead %}{% if lead %}{{ lead }}{% endif %}{% endblock %}{% endset %}
  {% if lead_content is not empty %}
    <div class="form__lead flex flex-wrap items-center gap-[6px] pb-[12px] border-b border-border-2"
      {%- if lead_label %} role="group" aria-label="{{ lead_label }}"{% endif %}>
      {{ lead_content }}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
  {% block lines %}{% if lines %}{{ lines }}{% endif %}{% endblock %}
  {#- ONE ROW THAT WRAPS. `flex-wrap` and not a grid and not a scroller: the pills
      keep their intrinsic widths and fall to a second line when they run out of
      room, which is what keeps a long row from becoming either a column of
      controls or a horizontal scroll nobody finds. The group name is fixed rather
      than passed, for the reason above — the row is a row of this surface's
      properties, and there is nothing else it could be called. #}
  {% set pills_content %}{% block pills %}{% if pills %}{{ pills }}{% endif %}{% endblock %}{% endset %}
  {% if pills_content is not empty %}
    <div class="form__properties flex flex-wrap items-center gap-[6px]" role="group" aria-label="Properties">
      {{ pills_content }}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
  {#- THE ACTION ROW, right-aligned and separated, in the same treatment the hull
      gives its own footer — so a form that is its own surface and a form inside a
      dialog do not disagree about where the submit is. #}
  {% set actions_content %}{% block actions %}{% if actions %}{{ actions }}{% endif %}{% endblock %}{% endset %}
  {% if actions_content is not empty %}
    <div class="form__actions flex items-center justify-end gap-[8px] pt-[12px] mt-[10px] border-t border-border-2">
      {{ actions_content }}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>
